---
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { dataset } from '../lib/jsonld.js';
import { courtListener, formatCount } from '../lib/collection.js';
import { BOOT_TIMEOUT_MS, bootGuardScript } from '../lib/bootguard.js';
import '../styles/theme.css';
import '../styles/layout.css';
import '../styles/data.css';
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
		<title>Index data · stare.pub</title>
		<meta name="description" content="Every published rebuild of the stare.pub citation index: cases indexed, volumes, coverage and exceptions over time." />
		<meta property="og:title" content="§tare.pub index data" />
		<meta property="og:description" content="Every published rebuild of the stare.pub citation index: cases indexed, volumes, coverage and exceptions over time." />
		<meta property="og:type" content="website" />
		<meta property="og:url" content="https://stare.pub/data" />
		<meta property="og:image" content="https://stare.pub/og-image.png" />
		<JsonLd data={dataset()} />
		<script is:inline>
			try {
				var theme = localStorage.getItem('stare-theme');
				if (theme === 'dark' || (!theme && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches)) {
					document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'dark');
				}
			} catch(e) {}
		</script>
		<!-- If the charts' module fails to load, say so instead of "Loading" (#265). -->
		<script is:inline set:html={bootGuardScript({ targets: ['data-root'], what: 'This page\u2019s charts', timeout: BOOT_TIMEOUT_MS })}></script>
	</head>
	<body>
		<div class="page-topbar"><a href="/">§tare.pub</a><button class="page-theme-toggle" id="page-theme-btn" title="Toggle dark mode">&#9790;</button></div>
		<div class="page page--data">
			<h1>Index data</h1>
			<p>Each time the citation index is rebuilt, the build writes a report: how many cases and volumes it indexed, how many index entries it wrote for them, and every volume that failed or was let through with a known reason. This page charts every published report, from <a href="https://idx.stare.pub/reports/index.json">idx.stare.pub/reports/index.json</a>, and lists the live build&rsquo;s numbers by reporter.</p>
			<div id="data-root" class="data-root" aria-live="polite">
				<p class="data-note">Loading the index reports&hellip;</p>
				<noscript><p class="data-note">This page needs JavaScript to draw the charts. The reports themselves are plain JSON at idx.stare.pub/reports/index.json.</p></noscript>
			</div>

			<h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
			<p>The live index, {courtListener.index_version}, is built from two sources, which its manifest names in <code>based_on</code>: the <a href="https://case.law/">Caselaw Access Project</a>&rsquo;s case files (CC0 1.0), and <a href="https://www.courtlistener.com/help/api/bulk-data/">CourtListener&rsquo;s bulk data</a>, published by <a href="https://free.law/">Free Law Project</a>, from its export of {courtListener.export}. CourtListener marks its bulk data with the <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/">Public Domain Mark 1.0</a>, &ldquo;free of known copyright restrictions.&rdquo; From CourtListener, stare.pub takes the opinions and metadata of cases CAP doesn&rsquo;t hold, and the parallel citations in its citation table.</p>

			<h2 id="files">The files</h2>
			<p>Each volume&rsquo;s index is one gzipped text file, <code>idx.stare.pub/v/&lt;version&gt;/&lt;slug&gt;/&lt;volume&gt;.idx.gz</code>, with a line per case: <code>page,source,ref</code>. Lines sharing a page keep CAP&rsquo;s case first.</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>cap</code> lines name a case file in the volume&rsquo;s zip at <code>data.stare.pub/cap/&lt;slug&gt;/&lt;volume&gt;.zip</code>: <code>647,cap,0647-01</code> in <code>a2d/31.idx.gz</code> is <code>json/0647-01.json</code> in <code>a2d/31.zip</code>.</li>
				<li><code>cl</code> lines name a CourtListener record by cluster id and hash: <code>1,cl,4893133/62f253f296600fb7</code> in <code>f-4th/1.idx.gz</code> is <a href="https://data.stare.pub/cl/c/4893133/62f253f296600fb7.json.gz"><code>data.stare.pub/cl/c/4893133/62f253f296600fb7.json.gz</code></a>. data.stare.pub has {formatCount(courtListener.records)} of them, from CourtListener&rsquo;s export of {courtListener.export}.</li>
				<li>Aliases are <code>cap</code> lines naming a case in another volume, with <code>key=value</code> fields after it. <code>via=cl:&lt;cluster&gt;</code> means CourtListener&rsquo;s citation table links the two citations: <code>287,cap,neb/128/0783-01,via=cl:8077312</code> in <code>n-w/260.idx.gz</code> files 128 Neb. 783 under its parallel citation, 260 N.W. 287. The site labels a case reached this way &ldquo;Resolved through CourtListener&rsquo;s citation metadata.&rdquo;</li>
			</ul>
			<p>A CourtListener record is gzipped JSON, served as <code>application/gzip</code> with no <code>Content-Encoding</code>, and never changes once published. It has the shape of a CAP case (<code>name</code>, <code>name_abbreviation</code>, <code>decision_date</code>, <code>citations</code>, <code>court</code>, <code>jurisdiction</code> and <code>casebody</code>) plus a <code>source</code> block with CourtListener&rsquo;s ids, the export date, the docket number, the precedential status and the case&rsquo;s URL on CourtListener. Each record states its own terms: <code>license</code> is the Public Domain Mark 1.0 (<code>CC-PDM-1.0</code>), and <code>attribution</code> names CourtListener, its publisher Free Law Project, and the export the record came from. The case viewer shows that source on the case, linked to the opinion on CourtListener. CourtListener records have no page scans.</p>
			<p><a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> walks through resolving a citation with these files, and <a href="/openapi.json">openapi.json</a> describes each of them.</p>

			<h2 id="licences">Licences</h2>
			<p>The Caselaw Access Project case files on data.stare.pub are <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">CC0 1.0</a>, the terms CAP published them under. Everything else stare.pub produces, including the citation index, the build reports charted here, the reporter map, the OpenAPI document and schemas, and the case pages, is <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">CC BY-NC-ND 4.0</a>; credit stare.pub (https://stare.pub), the authoritative source. Most of the reporter map&rsquo;s names, variations and editions come from Free Law Project&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/freelawproject/reporters-db">reporters-db</a>, and most of the citare data bundle from reporters-db and <a href="https://github.com/freelawproject/courts-db">courts-db</a>; those parts are under their <a href="https://spdx.org/licenses/BSD-2-Clause.html">BSD 2-Clause</a> licence (Copyright (c) 2014 and 2020, Free Law Project). The rest come from <a href="https://github.com/codexpublicus/citare">citare</a>&rsquo;s own supplements to reporters-db, under the project&rsquo;s CC BY-NC-ND 4.0. reporters.json&rsquo;s <code>sources</code> and the bundle&rsquo;s sidecar <code>citare-data-&lt;hash&gt;.json</code> list every source, one entry each, with what it contributes, and reproduce the BSD licence. Each CourtListener record under data.stare.pub/cl/ carries, in <code>license</code>, the <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/">Public Domain Mark 1.0</a> CourtListener puts on its bulk data, and in <code>attribution</code> credits CourtListener (Free Law Project) and the export it came from. Each JSON file names its licence, and so does this page&rsquo;s JSON-LD.</p>

			<a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Back to stare.pub</a>

			<SiteFooter />
		</div>
	<script is:inline type="module">
			import { mountDataPage } from '/js/datapage.js';
			globalThis.stareBoot?.ready();
			mountDataPage(document.getElementById('data-root'));
		</script>
	<script is:inline>
			(function() {
				var btn = document.getElementById('page-theme-btn');
				if (!btn) return;
				try { if (localStorage.getItem('stare-theme') === 'dark' || (!localStorage.getItem('stare-theme') && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches)) btn.textContent = '\u2600'; } catch(e) {}
				btn.addEventListener('click', function(e) {
					e.preventDefault();
					var isDark = document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-theme') === 'dark';
					if (isDark) { document.documentElement.removeAttribute('data-theme'); btn.textContent = '\u263E'; try { localStorage.setItem('stare-theme', 'light'); } catch(e) {} }
					else { document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'dark'); btn.textContent = '\u2600'; try { localStorage.setItem('stare-theme', 'dark'); } catch(e) {} }
				});
			})();
		</script>
	</body>
</html>
